{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/uast.cljc'","aggregated_output":"(ns yang.python.antlr.uast\n  \"The Python lowering's name for `yang.core.uast` (docs/design/yang.typescript.md\n   §17, lift L2): every var is the shared one.\"\n  (:require\n    [yang.core.uast :as core]))\n\n\n(def lit core/lit)\n\n\n(def v core/v)\n\n\n(def lam core/lam)\n\n\n(def app core/app)\n\n\n(def if-node core/if-node)\n\n\n(def capture core/capture)\n\n\n(def def! core/def!)\n\n\n(def discard core/discard)\n\n\n(def then core/then)\n\n\n(def seq-nodes core/seq-nodes)\n\n\n(def let1 core/let1)\n\n\n(def sexp->uast core/sexp->uast)\n\n\n(def mark-tails core/mark-tails)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '38,45p' src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/safepoint.cljc; sed -n '1,35p' src/cljc/yang/tails.cljc; sed -n '50,70p' src/cljc/yang/antlr/packet.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"  (:require\n    [yang.core.prelude :as core]\n    [yang.core.uast :as u]\n    [yang.typescript.antlr.prelude :as prelude]))\n\n\n(def profile\n  \"The `yang.safepoint` profile this prelude implements under the bundled\n(ns yang.tails\n  \"Tail marks over the Universal AST, shared by every stage that builds or\n   rewrites a tree. A tail call pushes no frame on the VMs that honour the\n   mark, so a mark is only sound for the tree it was computed over: a stage\n   that wraps a body must strip every mark and recompute them over the whole\n   derived tree, or a wrapped body's old tail calls would skip the wrapper.\"\n  (:require\n    [yin.vm :as vm]))\n\n\n(defn mark-tails\n  \"Set `:tail? true` on every application in tail position of a lambda body,\n   through `if` branches. Definitions are never marked. Lowering relies on\n   this for loops, which are self-applied lambdas: the recursive call and the\n   sequencing lambda around it are both tail calls, so an iteration grows no\n   continuation on the VMs that honour the mark.\"\n  [node]\n  (letfn [(walk\n            [node tail?]\n            (case (:type node)\n              :lambda (assoc node :body (walk (:body node) true))\n              :if (assoc node\n                         :test (walk (:test node) false)\n                         :consequent (walk (:consequent node) tail?)\n                         :alternate (walk (:alternate node) tail?))\n              :application\n              (let [definition? (= 'yin/def (:name (:operator node)))\n                    node (assoc node\n                                :operator (walk (:operator node) false)\n                                :operands (mapv #(walk % false)\n                                                (:operands node)))]\n                (if (and tail? (not definition?))\n                  (assoc node :tail? true)\n                  node))\n              node))]\n     7. :yang.cst/grammar names the grammar revision and tool; the target does\n        not appear in the packet. Target equivalence is the contract.\n\n   Portable and language-agnostic: child edges are data, so the tree is\n   validated before anything walks it.\"\n  (:require\n    [dao.host.text :as text]))\n\n\n(def packet-version 1)\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Source text\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn code-points\n  \"The code points of `s` as a vector of integers (`dao.host.text`: a\n   surrogate pair is one code point, an unpaired surrogate its own).\"\n  [s]\n  (text/code-points s))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
